4.text:
For one endogenous regressor and one instrument write the first stage
as $x_{i}=\pi z_{i}+v_{i}$ and put $S_{zz}=n^{-1}\sum z_{i}^{2}$.  With
$\sigma_{v}^{2}= {\rm Var}(v)$

\begin{equation*}
F_{1}= \frac{\hat\pi^{2}S_{zz}}{\hat\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;\xrightarrow{p}\;
\frac{\pi^{2}S_{zz}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\equiv F_{0}\;,
\qquad 
F_{0}= \frac{n\pi^{2}E[z^{2}]}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with $A_{n}=n^{-1}\sum z_{i}\varepsilon_{i}$ and
$B_{n}=n^{-1}\sum z_{i}v_{i}$,

\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta=\frac{A_{n}}{\pi S_{zz}+B_{n}}
      =\frac{A_{n}}{\pi S_{zz}}-\frac{A_{n}B_{n}}{(\pi S_{zz})^{2}}+o_{p}(n^{-1}) .
\end{equation*}
Hence, to first order,

\begin{equation*}
E\!\left[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\right]\simeq
-\frac{E[ A_{n}B_{n}]}{(\pi S_{zz})^{2}}
=-\frac{\sigma_{z}^{2}\sigma_{\varepsilon v}}{n\pi^{2}\sigma_{z}^{4}}
=-\frac{\sigma_{\varepsilon v}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}\;
\frac{\sigma_{v}^{2}}{n\pi^{2}\sigma_{z}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  with the usual OLS (a.s.) bias
$\,b_{OLS}=\sigma_{\varepsilon v}/ \sigma_{v}^{2}$, 

\begin{equation*}
\frac{ E[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]}{b_{OLS}}
\;\approx\; -\frac{1}{F_{0}}\;,\qquad 
\Bigl|\frac{{\rm bias}_{2SLS}}{{\rm bias}_{OLS}}\Bigr|
\approx\frac{1}{F_{0}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular, heuristically
${\rm rel\;bias}\approx F_{0}^{-1}$.  The conventional $10\%$ rule
$F_{0}>10$ is therefore $E[{\rm rel\;bias}]<0.10$.

Let $t=\sqrt{F_{1}}$.  Under homoskedasticity
$t=\hat\pi/SE(\hat\pi)$ is asymptotically
$t\sim N\!\left(\sqrt{F_{0}},1\right)$, i.e. $\sqrt{F_{0}}$ is
asymptotically normal with mean $\sqrt{F_{1}}$ and variance one.
A $95\%$ two-sided lower confidence bound for $\sqrt{F_{0}}$ is

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{0}}^{L}=\sqrt{F_{1}}-z_{0.975}\simeq\sqrt{F_{1}}-1.96 .
\end{equation*}
We want the bound on the relative bias to be below $0.10$, i.e.
$F_{0}^{L}>10$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{1}}-1.96>\sqrt{10}
\iff\sqrt{F_{1}}>\sqrt{10}+1.96\approx3.16+1.96=5.12,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
F_{1}^{*}>\bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.96\bigr)^{2}\approx 26.2\approx26 .
\end{equation*}
In one–sided $95\%$ notation $z_{0.95}=1.645$ and

\begin{equation*}
F_{1}^{*}> \bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.645\bigr)^{2}\approx 23.1\approx 23,
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $F\approx 23\!-\!26$, the familiar Staiger–Stock $F>10$
rule inflated by the sampling uncertainty in $\hat\pi$.  With the
customary two-sided $1.96$ normal critical value this is normally
quoted as $F\gtrsim 16\!-\!20$ in the Stock–Yogo tables, $\approx 26$
on the simple $F^{-1}$-normal approximation, essentially the
$F\approx 10$ rule up to the $1.96\sigma$ safety margin.  The
one-sided $95\%$ form is $F^{*}\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.645)^{2}\approx23$,
often rounded to $F\approx 25$.

6.text:
For a single endogenous regressor which is instrumented by a single
instrument let

\begin{equation*}
Y=Z\pi +v ,\qquad y=Y\beta +u ,\qquad 
{\rm var}(u)=\sigma_{u}^{2},\;{\rm var}(v)=\sigma_{v}^{2},
\end{equation*}
and put $Q_{Z}=n^{-1}Z'Z$.  With homoskedasticity

\begin{equation*}
   \mu^{2}= \frac{\pi'Z'Z\pi}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\end{equation*}
is the usual concentration parameter.  In the just identified case

\begin{equation*}
\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta=\frac{z'u}{z'Y}
          =\frac{a}{\pi+b},\qquad 
a=n^{-1}z'u,\;b=n^{-1}z'v ,
\end{equation*}
and, to first order,

\begin{equation*}
E\big[\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta\big]
   \approx -\frac{{\rm Cov}(a,b)}{\pi^{2}}
   =-\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{n\pi^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The probability limit of the OLS bias is 
$\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$ up to the $O(\pi^{2})$ term which can be
neglected in the weak instrument limit.  Hence, in the Nagar
approximation

\begin{equation*}
\frac{E\big[\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta\big]}
     {E\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]}
   \approx -\frac{\sigma_{v}^{2}}{n\pi^{2}}
   =-\frac{1}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
\mu^{2}= \frac{n\pi^{2}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
With one instrument $F_{1}\approx\mu^{2}/1$ up to the usual $+1$
correction, i.e. in the large sample limit

\begin{equation*}
\frac{E\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]}
     {E\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta\big]}
   \approx\frac{1}{F_{1}}\approx\frac{1}{\mu^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that on average

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Rel.Bias}\approx\frac{1}{F}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the familiar rule of thumb
$1/F<0.10\iff F>10$ is obtained.  With the usual $F-1$ finite-sample
correction
${\rm Rel.Bias}\approx1/(F-1)$ and the Stock–Yogo $5\%$ test of
maximal relative bias gives the conventional

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.10}\approx 10.8\approx 11,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the textbook $F>10$ rule.  In particular
$E\big[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]/E\big[\hat\beta_{OLS}
-\beta\big]\simeq 1/F$ and $F\approx 10\!-\!11$ makes the Nagar
mean relative bias about ten per cent of the OLS bias.  This is the
conventional $10\%$ relative-bias rule of thumb.

Remark.  If the $1/F$ bias is treated as random and a one-sided
$95\%$ lower confidence bound for $\mu^{2}$ is required, 
$\chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})$ is with probability $1$ the same as
$(\sqrt{\mu^{2}}+Z)^{2}$, $Z\sim N(0,1)$.  The $95\%$ upper quantile
under $\mu^{2}_{0}=1/0.10=10$ is
$c_{0.95}\approx\big(\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95}\big)^{2}\approx
(3.16+1.64)^{2}\approx 23$,
so that an observed first-stage $F\gtrsim 22\!-\!24$, about $23$ in
the normal approximation, delivers a conventional one-sided $95\%$
lower bound $\mu^{2}>10$ and hence a $95\%$ one-sided bound
${\rm Rel.Bias}<0.10$.  This is the conservative $95\%$–quantile
version of the $F>10$ rule. ∎

